#StreakQ  [#DP](https://leetcode.com/problem-list/dynamic-programming/) [#GameTheory](https://leetcode.com/problem-list/game-theory/)

We play from player 1 perspective and create a DP : dp[left][right] = p1score - p2score

Here left and right are, index of nums array. we use top down approach, to solve this.

The base condition: If left are right are same, we return nums[left]. since its the only value left.

Memo: if dp[left][right] exists, we return it

Now for computation: Take left number : so score willl be nums[left] - recursion(left + 1, right); Here left+1, right, means, p2 will take either from left + 1 or right, so we minus that

Same for right side. Now take max, since we want best for p1. dp[left][right] = max(leftside, rightside) and then we return dp[left][right]

we call this topDown with recursion(0, nums.length-1)

[Leetcode](https://leetcode.com/problems/predict-the-winner/)

```js
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {boolean}
 */
var predictTheWinner = function(nums) {
    let n = nums.length

    //playin from p1 perspective and subtracting opponent score from p1 score
    //ie the next move after taking left or right
    //and then taking max, to see in which move, we lost minimum score when moving.

    //dp[left][right] = MyScore - OpponentScore

    let dp = Array.from({length: n}, 
        () => Array(n).fill(undefined))

    const topDown = (left, right) => {

        if(left === right) return nums[left]

        if(dp[left][right] !== undefined){
            return dp[left][right]
        }

        let takeLeft = nums[left] - topDown(left+1, right)
        let takeRight = nums[right] - topDown(left, right-1)

        dp[left][right] = Math.max(takeLeft, takeRight)

        return dp[left][right]
    }

    return topDown(0, nums.length-1) >= 0
};
```

